Defining Arrays in JavaScript:
An array is a special variable that can hold multiple values in a single variable. Arrays are zero-indexed.

Ways to define an array:
1. Array literal: var arr = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
2. Array constructor: var arr = new Array('a', 'b', 'c');
3. Empty array then add: var arr = []; arr[0] = 'a';

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>JavaScript Array Demo</title>
  <script>
    window.onload = function() {
      // Define array using literal syntax
      var Fruit = ['apple', 'banana', 'mango', 'grape'];

      // Print entire array
      document.getElementById('arr').innerHTML = 'Array: ' + Fruit;

      // Print each element using for loop
      var output = 'Elements:<br>';
      for (var i = 0; i < Fruit.length; i++) {
        output += 'Fruit[' + i + '] = ' + Fruit[i] + '<br>';
      }
      document.getElementById('elements').innerHTML = output;
    };
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <h2>Fruit Array</h2>
  <p id="arr"></p>
  <p id="elements"></p>
</body>
</html>
```

