XMLHttpRequest (XHR) Object:
The XMLHttpRequest object is a built-in JavaScript object used to interact with web servers asynchronously. It allows web pages to send and retrieve data from a server after the page has loaded without reloading the entire page. This is the core of AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML).

Key methods/properties:
- open(method, url, async): Initializes the request.
- send(): Sends the request.
- onreadystatechange: Event handler called when readyState changes.
- readyState: 0=UNSENT, 1=OPENED, 2=HEADERS_RECEIVED, 3=LOADING, 4=DONE.
- status: HTTP status code (200=OK, 404=Not Found).
- responseText: Response as text.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>AJAX XMLHttpRequest Demo</title>
  <script>
    function loadData() {
      var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
      xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {
        if (xhr.readyState == 4 && xhr.status == 200) {
          document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = xhr.responseText;
        } else if (xhr.readyState == 4) {
          document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = 'Error loading data!';
        }
      };
      xhr.open('GET', 'data.txt', true); // async = true
      xhr.send();
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <h2>AJAX with XMLHttpRequest</h2>
  <button onclick="loadData()">Load Data</button>
  <div id="result" style="margin-top:20px; padding:10px; border:1px solid #ccc;">Click button to load data...</div>
</body>
</html>
```
Note: Create a 'data.txt' file on the server with some content. When button is clicked, XHR fetches data.txt asynchronously and displays in the div without page reload.

